  "expected": "A B-tree index keeps keys in sorted order inside a balanced tree, letting a database locate rows in a few steps instead of scanning an entire table. Each node occupies one fixed-size disk page and stores many sorted keys along with pointers to child pages. Because a single page can reference hundreds of children, the tree is wide and shallow, so three or four levels can cover millions of rows.\n\nA lookup starts at the root page. The database compares the target key with the keys stored there, follows the pointer for the matching range, and repeats this at each level until it reaches a leaf that references the row. Most systems use the B+tree variant, which keeps every entry in the leaves and links neighboring leaves in key order, so range queries descend once and then scan sideways.\n\nWhen an insert fills a page, the page splits in two and a separator key moves up to the parent. Every leaf stays at the same depth, so searches, inserts, and deletes all cost O(log n).\n\nP0_LONG_OK",
